 ## Approach
 
-Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. The approach for delivering committed changes is still open: five-second HTTPS polling, self-hosted WebSockets, or a hosted realtime service. All three can meet the required freshness target. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. Committed changes are pushed to browsers over self-hosted WebSockets. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required; the freshness goal is within 15 seconds under normal connectivity.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery are designed together around the WebSocket approach. The change store must assign each committed edit a monotone revision number and maintain a publication outbox so delivery can push and replay events. Delivery manages persistent socket sessions and pushes change events to connected browsers. Browsers maintain a WebSocket connection, track the last received revision, and re-request missed events after reconnect.
 
